Q: In VS Code, how can I toggle maximization of the current workbench part / editor group? A: <p>VS Code doesn't come with such a feature. Though there is <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/issues/196722" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ticket #196722 (<em>Maximize Workbench Editor</em>)</a> about it. You can show your support by giving it a thumbs up. You can subscribe to it to get notified about discussion and progress. Please avoid making noisy comments like &quot;+1&quot; / &quot;bump&quot;.</p>
<p>As for working around the current limitation, I think the trickiest part of attempting this with keyboard shortcuts is there's no mechanism to remember what was open before maximizing something and minimizing those other things away (such as the primary sidebar, secondary/auxiliary sidebar, and the panel area), i.e. when you unmaximize, how would you know whether to open up the secondary/auxiliary sidebar again? You could create shortcuts that just assume that you have some &quot;normal&quot; non-maximized combination of things that should be open when nothing else is maximized, but I'm going to try a hacky way using zen-mode, which does have such memories, and which hides sidebars. (if you don't care about the state of the sidebars or panel, just bind to the <code>workbench.action.toggleMaximizeEditorGroup</code> command)</p>
<p>Limitations (sorry that there are a lot. It might feel like I'm not getting much farther than the asker's attempt, but part of what I'm trying to also do here is give a better/deeper idea of what the limitations are so the reader is not left completely in the dark as to what can or can't be done):</p>
<ul>
<li>This solution relies on <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/77356279/11107541">the maximization capabilities of VS Code 1.84</a>, so if you're on an older version, you'll have to upgrade or find another way.</li>
<li>At the time of this writing, as far as I know, there's still no way to maximize the primary sidebar or the secondary/auxiliary sidebar. See also <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/76681921/11107541">Keyboard shortcut for making VS Code Explorer or other Views fullscreen?</a>.</li>
<li>Since this is a Zen Mode approach, the Zen Mode settings apply, including: <code>zenMode.hideLineNumbers</code>, <code>zenMode.hideStatusBar</code>, <code>zenMode.showTabs</code> (used to be <code>zenMode.hideTabs</code> pre-VS Code 1.84), and <code>zenMode.centerLayout</code>. You might also be interested in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/74272910/11107541">How to hide the VS Code bar with file name, breadcrumbs, and action buttons?</a>. I'm not aware of these toggles having commands that can be bound to keyboard shortcuts.</li>
</ul>
<p>I used <kbd>ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>b</kbd> because that's what I always use for random temporary stuff I'm trying. Replace it with whatever you want the key to be. Put this in your keybindings.json (open with <code>Preferences: Open Keyboard Shortcuts (JSON)</code> in the command palette):</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
    &quot;key&quot;: &quot;ctrl+b&quot;, // TODO
    &quot;command&quot;: &quot;runCommands&quot;,
    &quot;args&quot;: {
        &quot;commands&quot;: [
            &quot;workbench.action.maximizeEditorGroup&quot;,
            &quot;workbench.action.toggleZenMode&quot;,
        ],
    },
    &quot;when&quot;: &quot;(editorFocus || inSettingsEditor || inKeybindings) &amp;&amp; !maximizedEditorGroup &amp;&amp; !inZenMode&quot;
},
{
    &quot;key&quot;: &quot;ctrl+b&quot;, // TODO
    &quot;command&quot;: &quot;runCommands&quot;,
    &quot;args&quot;: {
        &quot;commands&quot;: [
            &quot;workbench.action.unmaximizeEditorGroup&quot;,
            &quot;workbench.action.exitZenMode&quot;,
        ],
    },
    &quot;when&quot;: &quot;maximizedEditorGroup &amp;&amp; inZenMode&quot;
},
{ // edge-case thing. edit to your taste.
    &quot;key&quot;: &quot;ctrl+b&quot;, // TODO
    &quot;command&quot;: &quot;runCommands&quot;,
    &quot;args&quot;: {
        &quot;commands&quot;: [
            // &quot;workbench.action.maximizeEditorGroup&quot;,
            &quot;workbench.action.exitZenMode&quot;,
        ],
    },
    &quot;when&quot;: &quot;editorFocus &amp;&amp; !maximizedEditorGroup &amp;&amp; inZenMode&quot;
},
{ // edge-case thing. edit to your taste.
    &quot;key&quot;: &quot;ctrl+b&quot;,
    &quot;command&quot;: &quot;runCommands&quot;,
    &quot;args&quot;: {
        &quot;commands&quot;: [
            &quot;workbench.action.maximizeEditorGroup&quot;,
            // &quot;workbench.action.toggleZenMode&quot;,
        ],
    },
    &quot;when&quot;: &quot;editorFocus &amp;&amp; maximizedEditorGroup &amp;&amp; !inZenMode&quot;
},
{
    &quot;key&quot;: &quot;ctrl+b&quot;, // TODO
    &quot;command&quot;: &quot;runCommands&quot;,
    &quot;args&quot;: {
        &quot;commands&quot;: [
            &quot;workbench.action.toggleZenMode&quot;,
            &quot;workbench.action.toggleMaximizedPanel&quot;,
        ]
    },
    &quot;when&quot;: &quot;panelFocus &amp;&amp; !inZenMode&quot;,
},
{
    &quot;key&quot;: &quot;ctrl+b&quot;, // TODO
    &quot;command&quot;: &quot;runCommands&quot;,
    &quot;args&quot;: {
        &quot;commands&quot;: [
            &quot;workbench.action.exitZenMode&quot;,
            // &quot;workbench.action.toggleMaximizedPanel&quot;, // apparently not needed :/
        ],
    },
    &quot;when&quot;: &quot;panelFocus &amp;&amp; inZenMode&quot;,
},
</code></pre>
<p>You may or may not need to put <code>&quot;runCommands&quot;</code> in your <code>terminal.integrated.commandsToSkipShell</code> setting.</p>
<hr />
<p>There's also the fact that the VS Code team is experimenting with the feature to have open editors in separate windows (see <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/77232931/11107541">this</a>), so perhaps you might find that satisfactory as a workaround (enable the experiment, drag editor to its own window, and fullscreen it).</p>

Q: Use custom Validators in CORRECT ORDER A: <p>You specified this <code>GroupSequence</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@GroupSequence({Default.class, PasswordGroup.class, ConfirmPasswordGroup.class})
public interface OrderedValidation {
}

</code></pre>
<p>With this, <code>@Validated(OrderedValidation.class)</code> will first try to validate everything with the <code>Default</code> group. The <code>PasswordGroup</code> and <code>ConfirmPasswordGroup</code> validations will be executed if and only if the previous validations (including the <code>Default</code> group) succeed.</p>
<p>If you want to always execute the <code>@Password</code> validation but only execute <code>@ConfirmPassword</code> if the former succeeds, you can use your <code>GroupSequence</code> but instead use the <code>Default</code> group for <code>@Password</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@GroupSequence({Default.class, ConfirmPasswordGroup.class}) // no PasswordGroup.class
public interface OrderedValidation {
}

</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Password // no groups=, let it use the default group
@ConfirmPassword(groups = ConfirmPasswordGroup.class) // use the group here
public class RegisterDTO {
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>// that one can stay as-is
@PostMapping(&quot;/registracija&quot;)
public String register(Model model, HttpServletRequest request, RedirectAttributes attributes,
    @ModelAttribute(&quot;dto&quot;) @Validated(OrderedValidation.class) RegisterDTO dto, BindingResult result) {
</code></pre>

Q: Remove buttons from PrimeNG PickList in Angular A: <p>for anyone trying to figure out this in 2026 I have a solution for you.</p>
<p>PrimeNg version: ^21.1.9</p>
<p>PrimeNg has <a href="https://primeng.org/passthrough" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Pass Through</a> which is a great way to customize your PrimeNg component.</p>
<p>At the top of each component documentation page there are several tabs, one of them is the Pass Through one where you can see how to customize every piece of each component<br />
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/9Qj6kIjK.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/9Qj6kIjK.png" alt="enter image description here" title="Documentation tabs highliting Passthrough tab" /></a></p>
<p>Inside that page you will find the names of the two components that contain the buttons, one for the source list and one for the target list<br />
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/CULCvsVr.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/CULCvsVr.png" alt="enter image description here" title="sourceControl is correct PT element" /></a></p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/7cBN6feK.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/7cBN6feK.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>After you have found what the names are (from the interactive menu), check the table under the interactive menu to understand what kind of element they are, most important thing to know if it is an HTML component or a PrimeNg component nested inside.<br />
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/byDAVKUr.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/byDAVKUr.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>Now that you know that both sourceControls and targetControls are just HTMLDivElements, create a passthrough variable to bind to the PicklistComponent:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>protected readonly hideDivPt: PassThroughOption&lt;HtmlDivElement&gt; = 
{
    hidden: true,
};

protected readonly picklistPt =
{
    sourceControls: this.hideDivPt,
    targetControls: this.hideDivPt,
};
</code></pre>
<p>Or just simply</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>protected readonly picklistPt = 
{
    sourceControls: {
      hidden: true,
    },
    targetControls: {
      hidden: true,
    },
};
</code></pre>
<p>Then just pass it to the PicklistControl template pt input</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;p-picklist
    [pt]=&quot;picklistPt&quot;
    ...
    &gt;
&lt;p-picklist/&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>And its done!</p>
<p>Hope this helps</p>

Q: ASP.NET Core is changing the PascalCase to camelCase A: <p>I knew the answer: by default, ASP.NET Core uses camel case for JSON property names (e.g., <code>minDate</code>, <code>maxInt</code>). This may not match your C# model's Pascal case property names (e.g., <code>MinDate</code>, <code>MaxInt</code>).</p>
<p><strong>Solution</strong></p>
<p>Set <code>PropertyNamingPolicy = null</code> to preserve the original C# casing (PascalCase).</p>
<p>Code example:</p>
<pre><code>// For MVC Controllers
builder.Services
       .AddControllers()
       .AddJsonOptions(options =&gt;
           {
               // Use PascalCase for property names (null = no transformation, keeps original casing)
               options.JsonSerializerOptions.PropertyNamingPolicy = null;
           });

// For Minimal APIs and OpenAPI schema generation
builder.Services
       .Configure&lt;Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.Json.JsonOptions&gt;(options =&gt;
           {
               // Use PascalCase for property names in OpenAPI schema (null = no transformation)
               options.SerializerOptions.PropertyNamingPolicy = null;
           });
</code></pre>
<p>Reference: <a href="https://www.mykolaaleksandrov.dev/posts/2025/12/aspnet-json-serialization-tuning/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.mykolaaleksandrov.dev/posts/2025/12/aspnet-json-serialization-tuning/</a></p>
